 "name": "Streatery Interfaces: Publicness and Restoration",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the scenario-based between-subjects experiment in He, Zhu, Deng, Zhang, Chen, Zhang, Chen & Song (2026), \"Streatery Interface Design for Healthy and Inclusive Streets: A Scenario-Based Experimental Study of Perceived Spatial Publicness and Emotional Restoration\", Sustainability 18(13):6927. Each participant first answers the basic-information questions (gender, age, occupation, monthly income, previous streatery experience), is then randomly assigned ONE of four SketchUp renderings of the same street that differ only in the streatery (outdoor dining) interface — boundaryless open, fully enclosed, flexible permeable (low planters) or hybrid covered (planters plus umbrellas) — and rates 4 perceived spatial publicness items (items 3 and 4 reverse-coded) and 11 restorative-experience items (fascination, being away, coherence, extent) on a 5-point agreement scale. Answers are keyed by interface; the paper compared groups with Welch's ANOVA and Games–Howell tests and tested publicness as a mediator (N = 420, southern Chinese cities). Stimuli: the four renderings cropped from Figure 2 of the paper (CC BY 4.0); question wording from the English questionnaire in the paper's Supplementary File S1. The template will be removed if the authors object. Differences from the original study: (1) the interface is drawn with simple random assignment in the page, so group sizes are not forced to be equal; (2) the \"0–18 years old\" age option of the original questionnaire is left out because the consent form invites adults only; (3) all 15 items are shown on one page beside the image instead of the original online questionnaire layout; (4) no IP-based screening of respondents outside southern Chinese cities.",
 "author": "He, Y., Zhu, L., Deng, H., Zhang, N., Chen, Q., Zhang, S., Chen, X., & Song, C.",
